From fead70be44f6106501e694b584e1164301bfa9b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 03:40:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/19] wifi: rtl8192f-soc: add the driver scaffolding and the init
 tables

Add a mac80211 driver for the 802.11bgn 1T1R WiFi core integrated in
the Realtek RTL8197F router SoC.  The core belongs to the RTL8192F
(gen 2.5) family and is wired directly to the SoC bus, so neither the
PCIe plumbing of rtlwifi/rtw88 nor their descriptor layout applies.
The driver arrives as one patch per layer, each of which compiles and
links against the previous ones; the device only becomes usable with the
last one, which registers the platform driver and the mac80211
interface.  Everything this patch declares - the register and bit maps,
the descriptor layouts, the per-device struct and the function
prototypes - is consumed by the patches that follow, so none of it is
reachable yet.

This patch adds the scaffolding: Kconfig and Makefile, the driver
header with the MAC register map, the descriptor field layouts and the
per-device state, and the three register init tables that the bring-up
sequence consumes - MAC byte writes, baseband/AGC 32-bit writes and RF
register writes.  The tables are transcribed row-by-row from the
vendor PHYDM MP image for the 8197F.  That image is a
conditional-lookup format whose IF/ELSE markers select a board package
and silicon cut; for this SoC (package type 0, cut A, internal PA) the
vendor apply loop takes the ELSE branch of every block, so each table
here is that ELSE content in the original order.  RF rows at address
0xffe are the vendor 50 ms settling markers, which the apply code
turns into delays rather than writes.

Two entries are board-derived and say so in place: 0x4CA and 0x4CB cap
the aggregation number globally and bind whatever a TX descriptor
requests, and the value that works is 0x20 - what the running factory
firmware holds - not the 0x10 in the init image.

The driver takes its two SoC-side clocks from the clock provider
added earlier in this series; it never maps the system controller
itself.

No functional change is reachable from this patch: the tables are only
referenced by the bring-up code added later.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
